The live test reproduced a stalled uploader when remote MCP loaded the
same enrolled transport identity into a second network stack.

The shared adapter keeps one node per state directory under flock. Other
processes use tsnet's authenticated loopback SOCKS5 proxy. No LocalAPI
credential is shared. The owner closes the node before releasing its
lock. Borrowers can acquire ownership after normal shutdown or a crash.
The wire protocol, Device credential, and hub authorization stay intact.

Validation:
- The live-append regression fails before the fix and passes after it.
- Two MCP clients coexist with the uploader and survive owner changes.
- Both uploader and MCP ownership transfers preserve later appends.
- Revocation still denies remote queries after ownership transfers.
- make check and make tla pass, including the new ownership model.
- The adapter passes race detection and a macOS arm64 test cross-build.
- The rebuilt binary passes 30 live checks with isolated synthetic data.

Native macOS and cross-machine live behavior remain unverified. Stop
older device processes before upgrading because they do not acquire the
new ownership lock. Keep device state on a local filesystem.

